# The function does nothing with `arguments` but hand it to `apply`. Its generic entry walks the body and reads its own arguments where they lie: up to four are passed on by their count as a plain call; no list and no arguments object is made. More than four: the list is made in that arm.
# RUN: run-in-shell
# CHECK: <T/0///> <T/1/1//> <T/2/1/2/> <T/3/1/2/3> <T/4/1/2/3> <T/5/1/2/3> <T/9/1/2/3>
# CHECK-NEXT: <n/0///> <n/2/1/2/> <n/6/1/2/3> [<T/0///>] [<T/5/1/2/3>] </3/7/0/7> <c/1///>
# CHECK-NEXT: <i/0///> <i/2/1/2/> <i/6/1/2/3>
# CHECK-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(FORM): walk f2:GenericCallEntry mechanisms=all
# FORM-NOT: CreateRest
# FORM: GetArgument
# FORM: StrictEq
# FORM: CreateRest
# RUN(UNSEEN): walk f5:GenericCallEntry mechanisms=all
# UNSEEN-NOT: CreateRest
